          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lawrence-women-in-love-96fc35925c56519b\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":3,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":3},\"confidence\":0.92,\"analysis\":\"Birkin’s repeated whisper conventionally avows love and is explicitly spoken “with truth,” giving strong T support. Its setting within an already affirmed union gives slight P resonance, but the words do not primarily undertake a new commitment. Nothing independently presents them as a reflex-like discharge, so intensity and gladness do not establish E. Substantial O pressure arises because the narration explicitly treats the I–you love formula as inadequate to Birkin’s intended meaning: a transformation beyond separate selves and even “beyond love.”\",\"ambiguity\":\"The principal ambiguity is whether the transcendent meaning should be treated as an unusually metaphysical loving-state avowal within T or as genuinely residual core force under O.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":\"T can represent the surface avowal but not fully the utterance’s intended denial or transcendence of the separate loving subject and beloved object; P and E likewise do not capture the asserted emergence of a new, unitary being.\",\"core_not_context\":\"This pressure belongs to core force because the narrator directly glosses what Birkin means by these words, calling the conventional “I” a dead letter and stating that the utterance’s real truth is beyond love, rather than merely supplying external relationship context.\"},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Birkin directly whispers the repeated utterance “I love you, I love you” to Ursula; the narrator then interprets its intended force.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Birkin and Ursula have already treated themselves as effectively married and will marry legally the next day. Ursula hears an insufficiently demonstrative declaration, whereas the narration presents Birkin’s words as a truthful but radically inadequate formula for gratitude, rebirth, and a paradisal union in which separate “I” and “you” are transcended. Thus the interpersonal reception and the narrated intended meaning diverge sharply.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Even when he said, whispering with truth, ‘I love you, I love you,’ it was not the real truth.”\",\"supports\":\"The phrase is a sincere direct avowal supporting T, while the qualification supplies strong evidence that its conventional semantics do not exhaust its intended force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“It was something beyond love, such a gladness of having surpassed oneself, of having transcended the old existence.”\",\"supports\":\"Supports O by identifying self-transcendence, rather than an ordinary loving state, commitment, or reflexive discharge, as the crucial intended meaning.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“How could he say ‘I’ when he was something new and unknown, not himself at all? This I, this old formula of the age, was a dead letter.”\",\"supports\":\"Shows that the residual issue concerns the core first-person formula and its failure to represent transformed identity.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The wider passage explains that there was “no I and you,” but a “new, paradisal unit regained from the duality,” and says one cannot say “I love you” after both separate selves have been transcended.\",\"supports\":\"Clarifies the nonstandard, transpersonal force that creates genuine pressure beyond T/P/E.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Birkin says, “We’re married as much today as we shall be tomorrow,” and the couple are legally married the next day.\",\"supports\":\"Provides limited P resonance from the established marital undertaking, though the classified utterance itself does not clearly institute that commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e6\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is in Chapter XXVII of D. H. Lawrence’s Women in Love and is marked as a present affirmative explicit I–you love construction.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified occurrence and its conventional linguistic form.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"strained\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E captures the truthful surface avowal and slight relational resonance, but only O registers the passage’s explicit insistence that the formula means a transpersonal transformation beyond both ordinary love and separate personal identity.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":\"Transpersonal or mystical union that dissolves the ordinary speaker–addressee distinction.\"},\"notes\":\"E remains zero because whispering, trembling, passion, and gladness indicate affective intensity but do not independently show that producing the words is functioning as a reflex-like expressive discharge.\"}"
        }
